---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Readme"
  description="Ben Mayer's Readme — a user guide to my values and how I work."
>
  <div class="container readme-page">
    <section class="hero">
      <h1>Ben.Readme</h1>
      <p>A user guide on me and how I work. It captures my guiding values, how I work as a human, and will hopefully help you understand me better and help us work together.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="prose readme-body">
      <h2>North Star Ideals</h2>
      <h3>Do good</h3>
      <p>
        Good can mean different things: sometimes it's "positive social impact",
        sometimes "quality work", sometimes it's simply something "personally
        fulfilling". Do good, in whatever form good takes, and be intentional about
        it. When what you do aligns with what you value, magic happens.
      </p>

      <h3>Grow</h3>
      <p>
        Grow is important no matter how large or small. It comes from making
        mistakes and picking yourself up again. It asks for honesty and
        self-reflection, and is impossible if you believe you already know it all.
      </p>

      <h3>Enjoy the moment together</h3>
      <p>
        It's too easy to speed through life. Pause and practice gratitude for what
        you've achieved. Celebrate and laugh together. Life's short.
      </p>

      <h2>Product Values</h2>

      <h3>✨ Eliminate the non-essential</h3>
      <p>
        Put first things first. All the small things create noise that distracts
        from the essential and will result in not making an impact where it
        matters.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bad strategy is the active avoidance of making choices among competing
        values, whereas good strategy is focusing on what really matters and saying
        "No" to the other thousand non-essential things.
      </p>

      <h3>💡 You're not the customer</h3>
      <p>
        Listen to the customer, really understand their needs and problems that
        need solving. It's easy to assume you know what they want.
      </p>
      <p>
        Be curious, ask lots of questions, identify the need, validate, and ask
        again. Don't get attached to your ideas. You're not building a product for
        yourself, but for others. No ego.
      </p>

      <h3>⚡️ Speed is a necessity</h3>
      <p>
        It is absolutely crucial to be able to deliver quickly &mdash; where
        quickly doesn't mean rushing but being efficient. Understand that often you
        don't know if what you're building is going to work, so releasing quickly
        allows you to learn faster.
      </p>
      <p>
        However, releasing a feature is only the first step to solving the user
        need. You'll need to measure, analyse and iterate, fast and often.
      </p>
      <p>
        Perfection is the enemy &mdash; it's safe to fail, but fail quickly and
        learn fast. The path to a successful product is to outfail the
        competition.
      </p>

      <h3>🎉 Celebrate outcome</h3>
      <p>
        The problem with roadmaps is that they are project plans, and lead to
        measuring success by sticking to the plan as closely as possible.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rather than sticking to a carefully planned, prioritised list of features
        and their release schedule, create opportunity maps. Identify the biggest
        opportunities that will bring the highest business and user value. Commit
        to exploring them, and celebrate outcome rather than output!
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .readme-page {
    max-width: 42rem;
    padding: 0 1.5rem 4rem;
  }

  .readme-page .hero {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 4rem 0 2rem;
  }

  .readme-body h3 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
</style>
